        "text": "Likelihood-free acquisition functions intuitively model the belief of a candidate being promising. Instead of modeling the outcomes explicitly via a surrogate model p(y|x,DN ), Tree Parzen Estimators (TPE) (Bergstra et al., 2011) model two independent density distributions over the best and worst inputs, namely \u2113(x) = p(x|y \u2264 \u03c4,DN ) and g(x) = p(x|y > \u03c4,DN ). The density ratio (DR) serves as the acquisition function \u03b1: \u03b1DR(x;DN , \u03c4) = \u2113(x)/g(x). As discussed by Tiao et al. (2021), this approach suffers from several drawbacks due to solving the more challenging problem of density estimation as an intermediate step to the easier problem of estimating the ratio directly.\nTo fix these issues, Tiao et al. (2021) propose BORE, where the density-ratio is directly estimated by using class probability estimation. Theoretically, BORE can work with any classifier with probabilistic outputs, which enables us to use more robust and expressive algorithms. Let \u03b3 = p(y \u2264 \u03c4 |DN ) be the proportion of observations with top performing y values, and k the binary class label 1(y \u2264 \u03c4). BORE estimates the class label k for a given input x by a classifier C\u03b8 parameterized by \u03b8. They show that the prediction of C\u03b8 is proportional to the density ratio, \u03b1DR(x;DN , \u03c4) \u221d C\u03b8(x), if one minimizes a proper scoring rule (Gneiting & Raftery, 2007) w.r.t. \u03b8 to recover the true class-posterior probabilities:\nLBORE = \u2212 1 N N\u2211 n=1 (kn logC\u03b8(xn) + (1\u2212 kn) log(1\u2212 C\u03b8(xn))) (2)\nAlthough Tiao et al. (2021) argue that BORE resembles EI, assigning the same label to all observations with y < \u03c4 regardless of the magnitude of improvement over the threshold conforms to the definition of PI rather than EI, and might lead to conservative optimization with little global exploration (Garnett, 2022; Song et al., 2022). In practice, EI has proven to have stronger